  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and thunderstorms continue through this\nevening across most of the forecast area. Isolated strong to \nsevere thunderstorms are possible, with the greatest threat from\nany of these storms being gusty winds and small hail.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nShowers and storms have blossomed across the northern half of\nthe CWA, with frequent lightning found within the strongest of\nthe thunderstorms due to lower freezing levels found today.\nShear levels remain light, generally less than 30 kts, which is\npreventing further organization of these storms. That said, with\n25 kts of shear in the north, there remains the chance that\nstorms could organize enough for a brief period of small hail\nthis evening, or grow tall enough to collapse with gusty winds\n40 to 50 mph.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Fair weather this weekend will give way to more\nunsettled weather into early next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure returns Friday into this weekend, with drier\nweather and cooler temperatures returning to the forecast area.\nHigh temperatures each day may only lift into the 70s, and skies\ncould remain mostly clear Friday into Saturday. By Sunday, cloud\ncover may begin to increase once more ahead of the next\napproaching low pressure system, though there is the possibility\nthis system could slow upon approach, delaying the onset of\nmostly cloudy skies to early next week.\n\nThe next low pressure system to cross the area early next week\nwill be in the form of a cold front which brings another round \nof rain and chance for thunderstorms. Current trends suggest \nshowers will remain scattered along the boundary as they move \nthrough as there will be a lack of deep moisture support, but if\nthe trough deepens more and reaches further into the Gulf, there\ncould be more numerous showers and storms due to the synoptic \nforcing of the cold front.",
